.TH PLAY 1
.SH NAME
play, rec, vol \- play and record sound, the volume and the output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B play
.IR file.wav ...
.br
.B rec
.I file.wav
.RI [ seconds ]
.br
.B vol
.RB [ 0\-100 | mute ]
.br
.B vol out
.RI [ n ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
One program under three names, through the audio server,
.BR audiod (4)
(started when first needed). Ctrl-C stops
.B play
and
.BR rec .
.TP
.B play
Plays WAV files one after the other: 48 kHz, 16-bit, stereo PCM; others
are refused. A headset's or keyboard's media keys pause it, skip to the
next or previous file, or stop it.
.TP
.B rec
Records that long (5 s if not said, less than an hour) from the
microphone or line in into a WAV file of the same format.
.TP
.B vol
Shows the volume, or sets it (0-100; a number also unmutes;
.B mute
mutes).
.TP
.B vol out
Lists the outputs (0 the speakers, then each monitor with sound, then
connected Bluetooth headphones, by name;
.B *
the one playing), or plays to output
.IR n .
The choice lasts until audiod ends.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0; 1 on an error (not a WAV file, no sound card) or a usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
rec hello.wav 3
play hello.wav
vol 60
vol out 1
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR audiod (4),
.BR bt (1)
.SH BUGS
Only 48 kHz, 16-bit stereo PCM (no conversion, no surround). No
microphone choice (the first one wired) and no record level.
